# Hermes G2 Privacy Policy

Hermes G2 is a privacy-conscious app. It does not operate a user account or
billing service. The data path depends on the transcription and assistant
backends selected by the user.

## Audio and transcription

Hermes G2 captures audio for the voice assistant, Transcribe, voice typing into
Terminal, and other user-invoked voice features. On-device transcription is the
default. When it is selected, audio stays on the phone and is not retained
after processing unless the developer setting to save voice recordings is
enabled.

Users may instead provide their own API key and select Deepgram, OpenAI Whisper,
ElevenLabs, or Soniox. Audio is then sent to the selected service, associated
with the account that issued the key, and handled under that provider's terms
and retention policy. Hermes G2 does not transmit audio to an unselected
transcription provider.

- Deepgram: https://deepgram.com/privacy
- OpenAI: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
- ElevenLabs: https://elevenlabs.io/privacy-policy
- Soniox: https://soniox.com/policies/privacy-policy

## Hermes Agent bridge

The Hermes Agent bridge is the preferred/default assistant backend. After
transcription, Hermes G2 sends the utterance text and limited turn context to
the bridge host configured by the user. Context can include the foreground app
and title, local time, screen state, and headset battery. The phone also exposes
its current glasses tools to that bridge over MCP. Tool results can contain data
such as screen descriptions, notifications, calendar events, media state,
location-derived information, or text from an open app when a requested tool
has access to it.

The bridge host and its Hermes Agent may process, retain, or forward this data
according to the user's own Hermes Agent configuration, model provider, tools,
and host security. Hermes G2 cannot control retention after data reaches that
host. The user supplies the host and shared token. The bridge connection uses
plain websocket transport and is intended for a trusted private network such as
a tailnet; the user is responsible for network access controls.

If proactive Hermes actions are enabled, Hermes Agent can call eligible glasses
tools outside a voice conversation. Hermes G2 enforces the local setting,
proactive-safe tool flags, and a rate limit. The assistant can still take
consequential actions or share information available to its tools; users are
responsible for the instructions and access they grant it.

## Direct-provider fallback

When the user selects the direct-provider fallback, Hermes G2 sends assistant
prompts, responses, tool results, and relevant turn context directly from the
phone to the selected Anthropic or OpenAI service using the user's API key. If
the downloaded on-phone model is selected instead, that assistant inference
stays on the phone. Conversation history and memory data may be stored locally.

- OpenAI: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
- Anthropic: https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy

## Notifications, calendar, media, and files

Hermes G2 can read Android notifications, calendar events, media metadata, and
user-accessible files when the user grants the corresponding Android access.
The app uses this data to render glasses interfaces and fulfill assistant tool
calls. Data returned through an assistant tool follows the selected assistant
backend's path described above.

## Location

The Navigate app uses precise location and a user-provided Mapbox token for
maps, geocoding, and directions. While Navigate is active, location and the
selected destination are sent to Mapbox under its privacy policy:

- Mapbox: https://www.mapbox.com/legal/privacy

The Weather app obtains coarse location and queries a third-party weather data
provider. Location may also be available to an explicitly invoked assistant
tool.

## Secrets and local storage

API keys, bridge tokens, connection settings, conversation state, and app
preferences are stored on the phone. Configuration helper scripts can extract
these values in plain text for development; their output must be treated as a
secret and must not be committed, logged, or shared.
